    },
    {
      "id": "6U6T5l1r9dCn0x3ijAQPz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -13526.986471366177,
      "y": -1636.959651417694,
      "width": 2134.49853515625,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "H9y16vf0bFn1oQs46X0Es"
      ],
      "frameId": "CS4m5clLlJch_PMWiidFi",
      "index": "aUG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 722325904,
      "version": 232,
      "versionNonce": 300690356,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2.7 The Universal Pattern: From nD to 1D\nWhether you’re working with 1D vectors, 2D matrices, 3D tensors, or even higher-dimensional data, the pattern remains the same:\n\nCalculate your thread’s unique coordinates using blockIdx and threadIdx\nApply boundary checks to ensure you don’t access invalid memory\nLinearize the nD coordinates into a row-major 1D memory index using stride calculations (this matches the layout used by C, NumPy, and PyTorch)\nPerform your computation on the data at that index\nThat third step is often called \"linearizing\" or \"flattening\" your index. Because PyTorch, NumPy, and plain C default to row-major layout, the stride formulas you’ve seen throughout this chapter follow that convention.\n\nThis is the foundation of all CUDA programming. Master this pattern, and you can parallelize operations on data of any dimensionality.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2.7 The Universal Pattern: From nD to 1D\nWhether you’re working with 1D vectors, 2D matrices, 3D tensors, or even higher-dimensional data, the pattern remains the same:\n\nCalculate your thread’s unique coordinates using blockIdx and threadIdx\nApply boundary checks to ensure you don’t access invalid memory\nLinearize the nD coordinates into a row-major 1D memory index using stride calculations (this matches the layout used by C, NumPy, and PyTorch)\nPerform your computation on the data at that index\nThat third step is often called \"linearizing\" or \"flattening\" your index. Because PyTorch, NumPy, and plain C default to row-major layout, the stride formulas you’ve seen throughout this chapter follow that convention.\n\nThis is the foundation of all CUDA programming. Master this pattern, and you can parallelize operations on data of any dimensionality.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
