      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2.5 Scaling Compute\n    - 1 block / 1024 threads, how many blocks can I have?1\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20.337163992374755,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2.5 Scaling Compute\n    - 1 block / 1024 threads, how many blocks can I have?1\n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2XmVFYgoHSctfqdFQOTfc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -13558.952343699266,
      "y": -1332.4127168698474,
      "width": 1760.7498024313747,
      "height": 254.21454990468445,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "O8uuvKM1YGIXSZliFBFix",
        "ahzjqX89uZJ14so78Gftz",
        "H9y16vf0bFn1oQs46X0Es"
      ],
      "frameId": "CS4m5clLlJch_PMWiidFi",
      "index": "aVV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 354027408,
      "version": 504,
      "versionNonce": 1760808628,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2.8 Summary\nA CUDA program consists of two main parts: host code that runs on the CPU and orchestrates the process, and device code (kernels) that runs in massive parallel on the GPU.\nThe terms Host and Device are the official CUDA terminology for the CPU and GPU, respectively. They have separate memory spaces.\nData must be explicitly transferred between host (RAM) and device (VRAM) memory using cudaMemcpy. This transfer is a potential performance bottleneck.\nGPU memory is managed with cudaMalloc and cudaFree, analogous to C’s malloc and free for host memory.\nA kernel is a C++ function marked with global that is launched from the host and executed by many threads on the device.\nCUDA organizes threads into a two-level hierarchy: threads are grouped into blocks, and blocks are organized into a grid.\nEach thread calculates a unique global index using built-in variables (blockIdx, threadIdx, blockDim, gridDim) to determine which piece of data it is responsible for processing.\nEssential C concepts like pointers (*) are fundamental to CUDA, as they are used to manage memory addresses on both the host and device.\nA simple CUDA program can be compiled from the command line using the CUDA compiler (nvcc).",
      "fontSize": 20.337163992374755,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2.8 Summary\nA CUDA program consists of two main parts: host code that runs on the CPU and orchestrates the process, and device code (kernels) that runs in massive parallel on the GPU.\nThe terms Host and Device are the official CUDA terminology for the CPU and GPU, respectively. They have separate memory spaces.\nData must be explicitly transferred between host (RAM) and device (VRAM) memory using cudaMemcpy. This transfer is a potential performance bottleneck.\nGPU memory is managed with cudaMalloc and cudaFree, analogous to C’s malloc and free for host memory.\nA kernel is a C++ function marked with global that is launched from the host and executed by many threads on the device.\nCUDA organizes threads into a two-level hierarchy: threads are grouped into blocks, and blocks are organized into a grid.\nEach thread calculates a unique global index using built-in variables (blockIdx, threadIdx, blockDim, gridDim) to determine which piece of data it is responsible for processing.\nEssential C concepts like pointers (*) are fundamental to CUDA, as they are used to manage memory addresses on both the host and device.\nA simple CUDA program can be compiled from the command line using the CUDA compiler (nvcc).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
