      "seed": 173839248,
      "version": 141,
      "versionNonce": 638940044,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Chp 5: Transformer inference in CUDA\nThis chapter covers\n    Understanding the decoder-only transformer architecture that powers GPT-style models\n    \n    The fundamental split between prefill (processing the prompt) and decode (generating tokens)\n\n    Building naive CUDA kernels for inference: GEMV, softmax, layer normalization, and attention\n\n    KV caching to avoid redundant computation during autoregressive generation\n\n    Mixture-of-Experts routing with custom TopK kernels\n\n    The CUDA-Python binding pipeline that connects our kernels to PyTorch",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Chp 5: Transformer inference in CUDA\nThis chapter covers\n    Understanding the decoder-only transformer architecture that powers GPT-style models\n    \n    The fundamental split between prefill (processing the prompt) and decode (generating tokens)\n\n    Building naive CUDA kernels for inference: GEMV, softmax, layer normalization, and attention\n\n    KV caching to avoid redundant computation during autoregressive generation\n\n    Mixture-of-Experts routing with custom TopK kernels\n\n    The CUDA-Python binding pipeline that connects our kernels to PyTorch",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "lZX9gHazwM3VnhmB0drXQ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -14227.355480552755,
      "y": 1391.8746842022329,
      "width": 1313.799072265625,
      "height": 1269.9769817447968,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "ah-LUpVdb3hy3whpdapTQ"
      ],
      "frameId": "CS4m5clLlJch_PMWiidFi",
      "index": "ak",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1696299920,
      "version": 1973,
      "versionNonce": 114711348,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5.1 Transfromer Architecture and Setup **inference**\n    - bottleneck is memory bandwidth not compute throughput because token parrallelism becomes matrix-vector products\n    - pyTorch baseline Cuda will match before optimizing / hybrid approaches where CUDA can optimize layers of PyTorch & Python\n\n5.1.1 Baseline Scenario (pyTorch)\n    - Training processes entire sequences in parrallel\n        - all 64 tokens flow through attention simultaneously\n    - Inference generates one token at a time depending on all previous tokens\n        - sequential dependency changes how we write CUDA kernels\n    - reference implementation uses: sequence = 64, embedding dimensions = 128, attention heads = 4, and 8 transformation layers\n        - position 32 can attend to positions 0-31 at the same time positino 40 attends 0-39\n            - parallel processing is what makes training a generalized matrix multiplication dominated workflow \n\n5.1.1 The Binding Pipeline: Connecting CUDA to Python\n    - CUDA kernels, C++ bindings, python wrappers\nFrom setup.py:\n    from torch.utils.cpp_extension import CUDAExtension, BuildExtension\n\nCUDAExtension compiles cuda to Pytorch's C++,\n    #include <torch/extension.h>\n    //It uses PyTorch’s extension header and pybind11 to expose functions:\n\n5.1.1 Dataset and Tokenization\n    - author uses The Wonderful Wizard of Oz by L. Frank Baum, a public domain novel that’s perfect for character-level language\nmodeling.\n    - Character-level tokenization treats each character as a separate token, creating a vocabulary of 65 unique characters (letters,\npunctuation, spaces, etc.).\n\n5.1.1 Decoder-only transformer architecture (GPT)\n    - encoder-decoder used for translations\n    - encoder-only used for classificaiton\n    - decoder-only used to generate text autoregressively: predict one token at a time, each prediction conditioned only on preivous\ntoken\n\nTransformer Terminology:\n    -> Head: A single attention computation that projects inputs to queries, keys, and values, computes attention scores, and\nproduces an output. Each head learns to focus on different relationships between tokens.\n    -> Multi-head attention: Multiple heads running in parallel, their outputs concatenated and projected back to the model dimension.\nThis allows the model to attend to information from different representation subspaces simultaneously.\n    -> Block (or layer): One complete transformer unit containing multi-head attention, a feed-forward network, and the residual\nconnections and layer normalization that wrap them. Our model stacks 8 of these blocks.\n    -> Model: The complete transformer: token embeddings, position embeddings, a stack of blocks, final layer normalization, and the\noutput projection to vocabulary logits (the raw, unnormalized scores for each token in the vocabulary before softmax converts them\nto probabilities).\n\nWe also need to be precise about tensor dimensions. Throughout this chapter, our primary activation tensor has shape (batch,\nsequence_length, embedding_dim) or (B, T, C) in code:\n\nB (batch): Number of sequences processed in parallel (16 in our setup)\nT (sequence length): Number of tokens per sequence (64 in our setup)\nC (channels/embedding dimension): Size of each token’s representation (128 in our setup)",
      "fontSize": 19.921207556781127,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5.1 Transfromer Architecture and Setup **inference**\n    - bottleneck is memory bandwidth not compute throughput because token parrallelism becomes matrix-vector products\n    - pyTorch baseline Cuda will match before optimizing / hybrid approaches where CUDA can optimize layers of PyTorch & Python\n\n5.1.1 Baseline Scenario (pyTorch)\n    - Training processes entire sequences in parrallel\n        - all 64 tokens flow through attention simultaneously\n    - Inference generates one token at a time depending on all previous tokens\n        - sequential dependency changes how we write CUDA kernels\n    - reference implementation uses: sequence = 64, embedding dimensions = 128, attention heads = 4, and 8 transformation layers\n        - position 32 can attend to positions 0-31 at the same time positino 40 attends 0-39\n            - parallel processing is what makes training a generalized matrix multiplication dominated workflow \n\n5.1.1 The Binding Pipeline: Connecting CUDA to Python\n    - CUDA kernels, C++ bindings, python wrappers\nFrom setup.py:\n    from torch.utils.cpp_extension import CUDAExtension, BuildExtension\n\nCUDAExtension compiles cuda to Pytorch's C++,\n    #include <torch/extension.h>\n    //It uses PyTorch’s extension header and pybind11 to expose functions:\n\n5.1.1 Dataset and Tokenization\n    - author uses The Wonderful Wizard of Oz by L. Frank Baum, a public domain novel that’s perfect for character-level language modeling.\n    - Character-level tokenization treats each character as a separate token, creating a vocabulary of 65 unique characters (letters, punctuation, spaces, etc.).\n\n5.1.1 Decoder-only transformer architecture (GPT)\n    - encoder-decoder used for translations\n    - encoder-only used for classificaiton\n    - decoder-only used to generate text autoregressively: predict one token at a time, each prediction conditioned only on preivous token\n\nTransformer Terminology:\n    -> Head: A single attention computation that projects inputs to queries, keys, and values, computes attention scores, and produces an output. Each head learns to focus on different relationships between tokens.\n    -> Multi-head attention: Multiple heads running in parallel, their outputs concatenated and projected back to the model dimension. This allows the model to attend to information from different representation subspaces simultaneously.\n    -> Block (or layer): One complete transformer unit containing multi-head attention, a feed-forward network, and the residual connections and layer normalization that wrap them. Our model stacks 8 of these blocks.\n    -> Model: The complete transformer: token embeddings, position embeddings, a stack of blocks, final layer normalization, and the output projection to vocabulary logits (the raw, unnormalized scores for each token in the vocabulary before softmax converts them to probabilities).\n\nWe also need to be precise about tensor dimensions. Throughout this chapter, our primary activation tensor has shape (batch, sequence_length, embedding_dim) or (B, T, C) in code:\n\nB (batch): Number of sequences processed in parallel (16 in our setup)\nT (sequence length): Number of tokens per sequence (64 in our setup)\nC (channels/embedding dimension): Size of each token’s representation (128 in our setup)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
