      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5.2 Inference: Theory and Optimization\n\n    - inference is fundamentally different from training.\n    - inference generates tokens one at a time through autoregressive sampling\n    - **APPLY KV-CACHING AND OTHER OPTIMIZATION**\n\n5.2.1 Optimizing Autoregressive Generation\n    - Inference flips our computational paradigm completley.\n        - instead of processing 64 tokens in parallel, we generate one token at a time (based on all\nprevious tokens; hence KV cache)\n    - previous training scale was for learning purposes but inference scaled to reflect production like\nworkloads (768 dimensional embeddings, 24 layers)\n    - at scale, we can better see the performance improvements using cuda kernel level optimizations\n\n5.2.1 KV Cache: Avoiding redundent computation\n    - KV cache stores key-value tensors from previous tokens during autoregressive generation,\navoiding recompute for each new token\n    - how does it work?\n         During each generation step, we compute the new token’s key and value vectors and append\nthem to our cache. When computing attention, the new token’s query attends to all cached keys and\nvalues, transforming the operation from GEMM (recomputing everything) to GEMV (one query against\ncached data).\n\n5.2.2 GEMV-Dominated Inference operations\n    - KV cache transforms computational patterns from matrix-matrix to matrix-vector\n        - we compute attention for one new position against all previous positions\n\nBecause T = 1 during generation (we process a single new token at a time), the returned tensor\nalready matches the narrow blue column. From there the attention heads run matrix-vector products\nagainst the cached keys and values instead of full matrix-matrix multiplies, so memory bandwidth\nbecomes the primary bottleneck.\n\n5.2.2 cache vs recomputation memory trade-offs\n    - KV cache trades memory for computation; compute only new ones\n        - The longer the sequence, the more computation we save, but the more memory we consume.\nThis trade-off becomes critical when serving models at scale.\n\n5.2.2 Mixture of Experts in Inference: Sparse Activation\n    - What is it? Mixture of Experts (MoE) is an architectural pattern that replaces a single dense\nfeed-forward layer with multiple parallel feed-forward networks called \"experts.\" Each expert has\nidentical architecture (the same layer sizes and activation functions) but different learned weights. A\nrouter network scores each expert for a given input and selects the top-k to actually run. The term\n\"expert\" is a metaphor from the machine learning literature: through training, each network tends to\nspecialize in different input patterns, becoming an \"expert\" at processing certain types of tokens.\nThis specialization emerges from optimization, not from explicit design - the router and experts co-\nadapt so that tokens get routed to whichever expert produces the best predictions. \n\nWhy should I care? MoE allows models to scale to massive parameter counts (trillions) while keeping\ninference costs manageable. Only a small fraction (typically 2-4 experts out of hundreds) are active\nper token, dramatically reducing compute requirements.\n\nHow does it work? For each token, a router network computes scores for all experts, selects the top-\nk highest scoring experts, normalizes their weights, and combines their outputs. During inference, this\nsparse activation means you’re only computing ~1% of the model’s total parameters per token.\n\nThe routing process involves several key steps:\n\nRouter Computation: A linear layer transforms each token’s embedding into expert scores\nSoftmax Normalization: Convert scores to probabilities across all experts\nTop-K Selection: Choose the k experts with highest probabilities\nLoad Balancing: Re-normalize probabilities among selected experts\nExpert Execution: Run each selected expert on the token\nOutput Combination: Weighted sum of expert outputs\nThis sparsity creates unique optimization opportunities for CUDA kernels, particularly in the top-k\nselection and routing computation. figure 5.10 illustrates the top-k selection process that determines\nwhich experts process each token.\n\n5.2.2 CUDA Optimized MOE Routing\n- hand the heavy lifting to CUDA kernels: score experts in PyTorch, offload the softmax and top-k\nwork to CUDA, then combine the selected expert outputs back to Python with a handful of tensors\n\n5.2.2 GEMV vs GEMM: the fundamental shift\n    Training Pattern (GEMM):\n    \n    Process 8 tokens simultaneously: [4,8,4] @ [4,4] = [4,8,4]\n    Attention computes full 8×8 matrices\n    High arithmetic intensity\n    All positions computed in parallel\n    Inference Pattern (GEMV):\n    \n    Process 1 new token: [1,1,4] @ [4,4] = [1,1,4]\n    Attention: 1 query against cached keys\n    Lower arithmetic intensity per step\n    Sequential token generation\n    Memory bandwidth critical for cache access\n\n\n5.2.2 Deploying the Optimized Stack\n    - All of the inference optimizations only matter if the end-to-end experience improves. \n... Complete Generation Example: Token by Token\nThis benchmarking framework measures the two critical inference metrics: Time To First Token\n(TTFT) for responsiveness and tokens per second for throughput. CUDA kernels primarily help with\nTTFT by reducing the overhead of individual operations.\n\nProduction Inference Results: PyTorch vs CUDA\nRunning our complete benchmarking suite shows the practical effect of CUDA optimization for\ntransformer inference. figure 5.12 shows the performance comparison.\n\nFigure 5.12 Performance comparison: our naive CUDA kernels underperform PyTorch’s optimized\nimplementations, demonstrating why production systems use battle-tested libraries\npytorch vs cuda perf\nlisting 5.23 demonstrates the thorough comparison and validation code that verifies correctness while\nmeasuring performance.\n\n\nRun all four implementations (PyTorch dense/MoE, CUDA dense/MoE) Require exact token-by-token\nagreement for the dense models Measure MoE divergence by token difference rate because routing can\ndrift under tiny floating-point perturbations\n\nThe inference.py comparison demonstrates exact token sequence matching for dense models and\ntolerance-based verification for MoE models. CUDA implementations show speedups ranging from 0.9x\nto 1.5x (some operations see overhead from CPU-GPU transfers in our educational implementation),\nwith MoE models benefiting more from the sparse routing optimizations at scale. The hybrid approach\nensures correctness while providing performance improvements through targeted CUDA kernel\nacceleration.\n\nThe actual model configurations used in inference.py:\n\nDense model: 95-token printable-ASCII vocabulary, 768 embedding dimensions, 8 attention heads, 24\nlayers, ~177M parameters\nMoE model: Same inference-oriented configuration, but with 8 experts using top-2 routing, ~708M\ntotal parameters (only ~177M active per token)\nNext Steps: Expanding CUDA Kernel Coverage\nOur inference optimization journey shows a clear progression:\n\nPhase 1: Prove Concept - Start with simple embedding kernels to establish the kernel binding pipeline\nand verify correctness.\n\nPhase 2: Target Bottlenecks - Profile to identify which operations consume the most time, then\nimplement CUDA kernels for those specific operations.\n\nPhase 3: Optimize Memory Access - For inference-heavy workloads, focus on memory bandwidth\noptimization, kernel fusion, and reducing data movement.\n\nThe foundation we’ve built with embedding kernels extends naturally to attention, MLP, and layer\nnormalization operations, each providing incremental performance improvements.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5.2 Inference: Theory and Optimization\n\n    - inference is fundamentally different from training.\n    - inference generates tokens one at a time through autoregressive sampling\n    - **APPLY KV-CACHING AND OTHER OPTIMIZATION**\n\n5.2.1 Optimizing Autoregressive Generation\n    - Inference flips our computational paradigm completley.\n        - instead of processing 64 tokens in parallel, we generate one token at a time (based on all previous tokens; hence KV cache)\n    - previous training scale was for learning purposes but inference scaled to reflect production like workloads (768 dimensional embeddings, 24 layers)\n    - at scale, we can better see the performance improvements using cuda kernel level optimizations\n\n5.2.1 KV Cache: Avoiding redundent computation\n    - KV cache stores key-value tensors from previous tokens during autoregressive generation, avoiding recompute for each new token\n    - how does it work?\n         During each generation step, we compute the new token’s key and value vectors and append them to our cache. When computing attention, the new token’s query attends to all cached keys and values, transforming the operation from GEMM (recomputing everything) to GEMV (one query against cached data).\n\n5.2.2 GEMV-Dominated Inference operations\n    - KV cache transforms computational patterns from matrix-matrix to matrix-vector\n        - we compute attention for one new position against all previous positions\n\nBecause T = 1 during generation (we process a single new token at a time), the returned tensor already matches the narrow blue column. From there the attention heads run matrix-vector products against the cached keys and values instead of full matrix-matrix multiplies, so memory bandwidth becomes the primary bottleneck.\n\n5.2.2 cache vs recomputation memory trade-offs\n    - KV cache trades memory for computation; compute only new ones\n        - The longer the sequence, the more computation we save, but the more memory we consume. This trade-off becomes critical when serving models at scale.\n\n5.2.2 Mixture of Experts in Inference: Sparse Activation\n    - What is it? Mixture of Experts (MoE) is an architectural pattern that replaces a single dense feed-forward layer with multiple parallel feed-forward networks called \"experts.\" Each expert has identical architecture (the same layer sizes and activation functions) but different learned weights. A router network scores each expert for a given input and selects the top-k to actually run. The term \"expert\" is a metaphor from the machine learning literature: through training, each network tends to specialize in different input patterns, becoming an \"expert\" at processing certain types of tokens. This specialization emerges from optimization, not from explicit design - the router and experts co-adapt so that tokens get routed to whichever expert produces the best predictions. \n\nWhy should I care? MoE allows models to scale to massive parameter counts (trillions) while keeping inference costs manageable. Only a small fraction (typically 2-4 experts out of hundreds) are active per token, dramatically reducing compute requirements.\n\nHow does it work? For each token, a router network computes scores for all experts, selects the top-k highest scoring experts, normalizes their weights, and combines their outputs. During inference, this sparse activation means you’re only computing ~1% of the model’s total parameters per token.\n\nThe routing process involves several key steps:\n\nRouter Computation: A linear layer transforms each token’s embedding into expert scores\nSoftmax Normalization: Convert scores to probabilities across all experts\nTop-K Selection: Choose the k experts with highest probabilities\nLoad Balancing: Re-normalize probabilities among selected experts\nExpert Execution: Run each selected expert on the token\nOutput Combination: Weighted sum of expert outputs\nThis sparsity creates unique optimization opportunities for CUDA kernels, particularly in the top-k selection and routing computation. figure 5.10 illustrates the top-k selection process that determines which experts process each token.\n\n5.2.2 CUDA Optimized MOE Routing\n- hand the heavy lifting to CUDA kernels: score experts in PyTorch, offload the softmax and top-k work to CUDA, then combine the selected expert outputs back to Python with a handful of tensors\n\n5.2.2 GEMV vs GEMM: the fundamental shift\n    Training Pattern (GEMM):\n    \n    Process 8 tokens simultaneously: [4,8,4] @ [4,4] = [4,8,4]\n    Attention computes full 8×8 matrices\n    High arithmetic intensity\n    All positions computed in parallel\n    Inference Pattern (GEMV):\n    \n    Process 1 new token: [1,1,4] @ [4,4] = [1,1,4]\n    Attention: 1 query against cached keys\n    Lower arithmetic intensity per step\n    Sequential token generation\n    Memory bandwidth critical for cache access\n\n\n5.2.2 Deploying the Optimized Stack\n    - All of the inference optimizations only matter if the end-to-end experience improves. \n... Complete Generation Example: Token by Token\nThis benchmarking framework measures the two critical inference metrics: Time To First Token (TTFT) for responsiveness and tokens per second for throughput. CUDA kernels primarily help with TTFT by reducing the overhead of individual operations.\n\nProduction Inference Results: PyTorch vs CUDA\nRunning our complete benchmarking suite shows the practical effect of CUDA optimization for transformer inference. figure 5.12 shows the performance comparison.\n\nFigure 5.12 Performance comparison: our naive CUDA kernels underperform PyTorch’s optimized implementations, demonstrating why production systems use battle-tested libraries\npytorch vs cuda perf\nlisting 5.23 demonstrates the thorough comparison and validation code that verifies correctness while measuring performance.\n\n\nRun all four implementations (PyTorch dense/MoE, CUDA dense/MoE) Require exact token-by-token agreement for the dense models Measure MoE divergence by token difference rate because routing can drift under tiny floating-point perturbations\n\nThe inference.py comparison demonstrates exact token sequence matching for dense models and tolerance-based verification for MoE models. CUDA implementations show speedups ranging from 0.9x to 1.5x (some operations see overhead from CPU-GPU transfers in our educational implementation), with MoE models benefiting more from the sparse routing optimizations at scale. The hybrid approach ensures correctness while providing performance improvements through targeted CUDA kernel acceleration.\n\nThe actual model configurations used in inference.py:\n\nDense model: 95-token printable-ASCII vocabulary, 768 embedding dimensions, 8 attention heads, 24 layers, ~177M parameters\nMoE model: Same inference-oriented configuration, but with 8 experts using top-2 routing, ~708M total parameters (only ~177M active per token)\nNext Steps: Expanding CUDA Kernel Coverage\nOur inference optimization journey shows a clear progression:\n\nPhase 1: Prove Concept - Start with simple embedding kernels to establish the kernel binding pipeline and verify correctness.\n\nPhase 2: Target Bottlenecks - Profile to identify which operations consume the most time, then implement CUDA kernels for those specific operations.\n\nPhase 3: Optimize Memory Access - For inference-heavy workloads, focus on memory bandwidth optimization, kernel fusion, and reducing data movement.\n\nThe foundation we’ve built with embedding kernels extends naturally to attention, MLP, and layer normalization operations, each providing incremental performance improvements.",
