      "updated": 1784678458619,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5.3 Inference Kernels\nInference has it's own performance profile: spceailize for single-token workloads where latency\n>>> throughput\n\n5.3.1 Memory layout and contiguity\n    -  **CUDA kernels expect contiguous memory**: this applies to training and inference\n    x_contiguous = [[1,2,3]\n                         [4,5,6]\n                         [7,8,9]]\n\n    x_contiguous_transpose = x_not_contiguous = [[1,4,7]\n                                                               [2,5,8]\n                                                               [3,6,9]]\n\n**torch.contiguous() #creates a new tensor with contiguous memory layout, costs one memcpy,\nbut ensures correctness\n\n5.3.2 GEMV: The inference workhorse\n    - Matrix-vector multiplication for single-token processing\nTraining uses GEMM (matrix-matrix): [B, T, D] @ [D, D] -> [B,T,D]\nInference uses GEMV (metrix-vector): [1, 1, D] @ [D, D] -> [1, 1, D]\n\nRemember, at inference, we process one token outputs as they arrive: GEMV (inference, 1 new\ntoken)\nGEMM (training, T=64 tokens):\nQ[64, 128] @ K.T[128, 64] = Scores[64, 64]\n-> 64 * 64 * 128 = 524,288 FLOPs\n-> High arithmetic intensity, compute-bound\n\nGEMV (inference, 1 new token):\nq[1, 128] @ K.T[128, 64] = scores[1, 64]\n-> 1 * 64 * 128 = 8,192 FLOPs\n-> Low arithmetic intensity, memory-bound\n\nGEMV vs GEMM kernel strategy\n- GEMM optimized for compute-bound arithmic intesity using shared memory tiling\n\n- GEMV is memory bound by loading, not computing the dot product\n\n5.3.2 topK for mixture of export (MoE) routing\nwhere k = 2, we select the 2 highest scorees\n\nTopK parallelizes across batch elements. Each token in the batch needs its own independent\nrouting decision, so we assign one CUDA block per token. Within each block, a single thread scans\nthrough all expert scores and maintains a small sorted buffer of the k best candidates.\n\n5.3.2 Design Decision: why naive insertion sort\n- Why this works for MoE:\n\nk is tiny (k=2 or k=4 typically)\nn is small (n=8 experts in our example)\nOverhead of parallel algorithms > benefit\nTotal comparisons: 8 * 2 = 16 (trivial)\nPerformance: For k=2, n=8:\n\nOur kernel: ~2 μs\nParallel reduction: ~15 μs (synchronization overhead)\nAs k grows (k=64 for larger MoE), you’d switch to parallel approaches. But for typical MoE (k=2),\nsimple wins.\n\nNote on alternative methods:\nOption 1: Parallel reduction (like finding max)\n\n// Multiple rounds: find max, mask it, repeat k times\n__shared__ float smax[256];\nfor (round = 0; round < k; round++) {\n    parallel_max_reduce(input, smax);\n    top_k[round] = smax[0];\n    input[argmax] = -INF;  // Mask found value\n}\n\nOverhead: thread synchronization, shared memory management Good for: Large k (k > 32)\n\nOption 2: Sorting networks\n// Bitonic sort: fixed comparison pattern\n// Compare-exchange pairs in parallel\nif ((i & mask) == 0)\n    compare_and_swap(arr[i], arr[i ^ mask]);\n\nOverhead: Complex indexing, works well for power-of-2 sizes Good for: k close to n\n\nOption 3: Insertion sort (our choice)\n// Simple: one thread scans all values\nfor each value in input:\n    if value > smallest_in_top_k:\n        insert_sorted(value, top_k)\n\nWorks best for: Small k (k=2 in our case)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5.3 Inference Kernels\nInference has it's own performance profile: spceailize for single-token workloads where latency >>> throughput\n\n5.3.1 Memory layout and contiguity\n    -  **CUDA kernels expect contiguous memory**: this applies to training and inference\n    x_contiguous = [[1,2,3]\n                         [4,5,6]\n                         [7,8,9]]\n\n    x_contiguous_transpose = x_not_contiguous = [[1,4,7]\n                                                               [2,5,8]\n                                                               [3,6,9]]\n\n**torch.contiguous() #creates a new tensor with contiguous memory layout, costs one memcpy, but ensures correctness\n\n5.3.2 GEMV: The inference workhorse\n    - Matrix-vector multiplication for single-token processing\nTraining uses GEMM (matrix-matrix): [B, T, D] @ [D, D] -> [B,T,D]\nInference uses GEMV (metrix-vector): [1, 1, D] @ [D, D] -> [1, 1, D]\n\nRemember, at inference, we process one token outputs as they arrive: GEMV (inference, 1 new token)\nGEMM (training, T=64 tokens):\nQ[64, 128] @ K.T[128, 64] = Scores[64, 64]\n-> 64 * 64 * 128 = 524,288 FLOPs\n-> High arithmetic intensity, compute-bound\n\nGEMV (inference, 1 new token):\nq[1, 128] @ K.T[128, 64] = scores[1, 64]\n-> 1 * 64 * 128 = 8,192 FLOPs\n-> Low arithmetic intensity, memory-bound\n\nGEMV vs GEMM kernel strategy\n- GEMM optimized for compute-bound arithmic intesity using shared memory tiling\n\n- GEMV is memory bound by loading, not computing the dot product\n\n5.3.2 topK for mixture of export (MoE) routing\nwhere k = 2, we select the 2 highest scorees\n\nTopK parallelizes across batch elements. Each token in the batch needs its own independent routing decision, so we assign one CUDA block per token. Within each block, a single thread scans through all expert scores and maintains a small sorted buffer of the k best candidates.\n\n5.3.2 Design Decision: why naive insertion sort\n- Why this works for MoE:\n\nk is tiny (k=2 or k=4 typically)\nn is small (n=8 experts in our example)\nOverhead of parallel algorithms > benefit\nTotal comparisons: 8 * 2 = 16 (trivial)\nPerformance: For k=2, n=8:\n\nOur kernel: ~2 μs\nParallel reduction: ~15 μs (synchronization overhead)\nAs k grows (k=64 for larger MoE), you’d switch to parallel approaches. But for typical MoE (k=2), simple wins.\n\nNote on alternative methods:\nOption 1: Parallel reduction (like finding max)\n\n// Multiple rounds: find max, mask it, repeat k times\n__shared__ float smax[256];\nfor (round = 0; round < k; round++) {\n    parallel_max_reduce(input, smax);\n    top_k[round] = smax[0];\n    input[argmax] = -INF;  // Mask found value\n}\n\nOverhead: thread synchronization, shared memory management Good for: Large k (k > 32)\n\nOption 2: Sorting networks\n// Bitonic sort: fixed comparison pattern\n// Compare-exchange pairs in parallel\nif ((i & mask) == 0)\n    compare_and_swap(arr[i], arr[i ^ mask]);\n\nOverhead: Complex indexing, works well for power-of-2 sizes Good for: k close to n\n\nOption 3: Insertion sort (our choice)\n// Simple: one thread scans all values\nfor each value in input:\n    if value > smallest_in_top_k:\n        insert_sorted(value, top_k)\n\nWorks best for: Small k (k=2 in our case)",
